# Gravitation 02 — From low Earth orbit to geostationary orbit
#
# (Halliday/Resnick ch. 13; Taylor §8.8; Kleppner & Kolenkow ch. 10.)
# Take GM⊕ = 3.986004418 × 10¹⁴ m³/s² and R⊕ = 6378.1 km.
#
#   (a) The radius of a geostationary orbit (period one sidereal day,
#       86164.1 s) from Kepler's third law.
#   (b) A satellite is in a circular orbit 300 km above the surface. Find the
#       two burns Δv₁, Δv₂ of a Hohmann transfer to geostationary orbit, the
#       total, and the transfer time.
#   (c) The Δv needed to escape from the 300 km orbit instead.
#   (d) Check (b) by integrating the orbit after the first burn: the apogee
#       should be at the geostationary radius, reached after the transfer time,
#       with the speed that the second burn tops up.

GM = 3.986004418e14 m³/s²
R_E = 6378.1 km
T_sid = 86164.1 s

# (a)
r_geo = ∛(GM T_sid² / (4π²))
print "geostationary radius:", r_geo in km to 6 digits
print "geostationary altitude:", (r_geo - R_E) in km to 6 digits

# (b)
r1 = R_E + 300 km
r2 = r_geo
v_circ(r) = √(GM / r)
a_t = (r1 + r2) / 2
v_peri = √(GM (2/r1 - 1/a_t))        # vis-viva on the transfer ellipse
v_apo = √(GM (2/r2 - 1/a_t))
Δv1 = v_peri - v_circ(r1)
Δv2 = v_circ(r2) - v_apo
t_transfer = π √(a_t³ / GM)
print "LEO speed:", v_circ(r1) in km/s to 6 digits
print "first burn:", Δv1 in km/s to 6 digits
print "second burn:", Δv2 in km/s to 6 digits
print "total:", (Δv1 + Δv2) in km/s to 6 digits
print "transfer time:", t_transfer in hr to 6 digits

# (c)
print "escape burn from LEO:", (√2 - 1) v_circ(r1) in km/s to 6 digits

# (d) fly it
solve r'' = -GM r / |r|³
  with r(0) = <r1, 0 km>, r'(0) = <0 km/s, v_peri>
  for t from 0 s to 1.5 * t_transfer
v_r(τ) = r(τ) · r'(τ) / |r(τ)|
solve v_r(t_apo) = 0 km/s for t_apo from 0.5 * t_transfer to 1.5 * t_transfer
print "ODE apogee radius:", |r(t_apo)| in km to 6 digits
print "ODE time to apogee:", t_apo in hr to 6 digits
print "ODE speed at apogee:", |r'(t_apo)| in km/s to 6 digits
